3. THIRD-PARTY TEXTS (not licensed by this project)
---------------------------------------------------

  data/labelled_sources.jsonl, data/train_sources.jsonl,
  data/holdout_sources.jsonl, data/snapshots/*.jsonl,
  and the Leapfrog-LSA/cats-snapshots repository.

These files contain headlines, summaries and, for some feeds, full article
bodies, copied from the publishers' public RSS feeds. The copyright belongs
to each publisher. This project grants NO licence to these texts, and none of
the licences above applies to them. Scale and per-publisher breakdown:
NOTICE.md section 1, or run research/text_inventory.py.

TODO (legal) — questions to settle before deciding whether these texts stay
public and before any Zenodo deposit:

  a) Basis for collecting and keeping them. Italian law has two
     text-and-data-mining exceptions, both from Directive (EU) 2019/790
     arts. 3-4 and introduced by D.Lgs. 177/2021:
       - L. 633/1941 art. 70-ter covers research organisations and
         cultural-heritage institutions.
       - Art. 70-quater covers anyone, with lawful access, unless the
         rights holder has expressly reserved TDM. It allows copies to be
         kept only as long as the extraction needs them.
     Which, if either, applies to this project? Have any of the publishers
     reserved TDM (opt-out)? Note also art. 70-septies (L. 132/2025) on AI.

  b) Publishing. The exceptions in (a) are about reproduction and
     extraction. Does any of them, or quotation under art. 70, cover making
     the texts PUBLICLY available in a git repository or a Zenodo deposit,
     especially the full article bodies (texts over 5,000 characters)?

  c) Other jurisdictions. Several publishers are outside the EU (for
     example dawn.com, mb.com.ph, thehill.com).

  d) Personal data in the texts. Assess GDPR Art. 85 (journalistic,
     academic expression) or another basis (roadmap, item 18).

  e) What to do now. The options include:
       - keep the texts as they are;
       - keep only hashes, lengths, timestamps and links, plus the derived
         signal values;
       - truncate the texts to headline + short summary;
       - make the texts private and purge them from git history.
     The purge is a history rewrite with a force-push and needs the
     maintainer's explicit approval (roadmap, item 2).

Takedown (PROPOSED, not yet in force): until this is settled, a publisher
who wants its texts removed could write to technical@cats-system.org, and
the texts would be removed from the current tree. Removing them from git
history needs the purge in (e).
